   "etymology": "From Proto-Albanian *əśkʲípe, from Pre-Proto-Albanian *əśkʲípii̯ā, from Proto-Indo-European *eǵʰs-kép-ii̯eh₂-, with a semantic change from \"that which chops (with a beak)\" to \"eagle\". Connected to qep (“beak”), kep (“to hew; peck”), and potentially to pjek (“to hit, touch”), all from the root Proto-Indo-European *(s)kep- (“to chop”) (compare Lithuanian kàpti, Ancient Greek κόπτω (kóptō)).\nMeyer's assumption of a borrowing from Latin accipiter is phonetically untenable (see qift). Orel's assumption from shkabë and a verb *shqipoj \"to seize\" must also be rejected, as shqipoj has only ever been attested meaning \"to speak clearly\".\nA borrowing from Proto-Celtic *sklubos (See Welsh ysglyf), which is metathesized from the better attested form *skublos, is also problematic because Proto-Albanian *-kl- is always retained in Old Albanian and often in dialects like Arbëresh and Arvanitika, but shqipe has never been attested containing -kl-.",
